{"page": 69, "text": "Total Grains segment revenues increased $596.3 million, or 2.8%, during the year ended August 31, 2026, compared to the year ended August 31, 2025, primarily due to the following: \u2022Higher feed grains sales volumes, driven by increased retail and wholesale Pacific Northwest activity, significantly increased Grains segment revenues. The favorable impact of higher volumes was slightly offset by lower feed grains selling prices. \u2022Lower oilseeds sales volumes, driven by lower Pacific Northwest volumes and planned downtime at our Fairmont and Mankato, Minnesota, processing facilities, partially offset the increase in revenues. However, this was partially mitigated by higher oilseeds selling prices. \u2022Higher wheat selling prices and increased sales volumes from our European operations also increased Grains segment revenues.\nGrains segment IBIT increased $83.4 million, or 189.5%, during the year ended August 31, 2026, compared to the year ended August 31, 2025, primarily due to the following: \u2022Higher oilseed crush margins during the second half of the fiscal year, driven by increased demand resulting from higher biofuels blending obligations, more than offset weaker crush margins during the first half of the fiscal year and increased Grains segment IBIT. \u2022Higher ethanol margins, driven by U.S. renewable energy policies, and increased feed grains export volumes further increased IBIT. \u2022These favorable impacts were partially offset by weaker retail and wholesale grain margins and continued volatility in global grain markets.\n37 Table of Contents 2025 Compared to 2024\nYears Ended August 31, Change\n2025 2024 Dollars Percent\n(Dollars in thousands)\nRevenues $ 20,987,888 $ 24,092,806 $ (3,104,918) (12.9) %\nIncome before income taxes $ 44,003 $ 254,570 $ (210,567) (82.7) %\nThe following commentary presents the changes in our Grains segment for the year ended August 31, 2025, compared to the year ended August 31, 2024.\nReve