nd Oklahoma made up the majority (greater than 50%) of this category.\n2025 2024\nStatutory federal income tax rate 21.0 % 21.0 %\nState and local income taxes, net of federal income tax benefit 2.4 0.5\nPatronage earnings (7.8) (12.6)\nDomestic production activities deduction (12.9) (6.8)\nExport activities at rates other than the U.S. statutory rate (3.3) 1.4\nIncrease in unrecognized tax benefits 2.8 2.7\nValuation allowance 2.8 (0.1)\nTax credits (4.3) (6.2)\nOther 2.0 (0.3)\nEffective tax rate 2.7 % (0.4) %\nThe primary drivers of the fiscal 2026 effective tax rate compared to fiscal 2025 are the decreased benefit of the cooperative \u00a7199 A(g) deduction, partially offset by a reduced unfavorable impact of state and local taxes, changes in unrecognized tax benefits and changes in valuation allowances. The primary driver of the fiscal 2026 effective tax rate compared to fiscal 2024 is a decreased benefit related to patronage earnings.\n"}
{"page": 168, "text": "CHS has determined that it qualifies for clean fuel production credits under Section 45Z of the Internal Revenue Code (\"45Z\"). 45Z provides a production tax credit for domestically produced transportation fuel that is produced after December 31, 2024, and sold before January 1, 2030. The amount of the credit is based on the fuel's carbon intensity score and compliance with applicable prevailing wage and apprenticeship requirements. CHS has elected to account for federal clean fuel production credits earned under 45Z as an income tax credit within the scope of ASC 740. Credits are recognized as a reduction of income tax expense in the period earned and included in the effective tax rate reconciliation.\nSubsequent to year-end, U.S. Treasury issued further guidance on 45Z which may affect the amount of tax credit we are able to recognize during fiscal 2027. We are continuing to evaluate the impact of this new guidance.\nWe file income tax returns in the U.S. federal jurisdiction, as well as various state and foreign jurisdictions. Our uncertain tax positions are affected by the tax years that are under audit or remain subject to examination by the relevant taxing authorities. In F-24 Table of Contents\naddition to the current year, fiscal years 2021 through 2025 remain open and subject to examination by the relevant taxing authorities.\nReserves are recorded against unrecognized tax benefits when we believe certain fully supportable tax return positions are likely to be challenged and we may or may not prevail. If we determine that a tax position is more likely than not to be sustained upon audit, based on the technical merits of the position, we recognize the benefit by measuring the amount that is greater than 50% likely of being realized. We reevaluate the technical merits of our tax positions and recognize an uncertain tax benefit, or derecognize a previously recorded tax benefit, when there is (i) completion of a tax audit, (ii) effective settlement of an issue, (iii) a change in applicable tax law including a tax case or legislative guidance, or (iv) expiration of the applicable statute of limitations. Significant judgment is required in accounting for tax reserves. A reconciliation of the gross beginning and ending amounts of unrecognized tax benefits for the periods is presented as follows:\n2026 2025 2024\n(Dollars in thousands)\nBalance at beginning of period $ 96,549 $ 65,115 $ 125,853\nAdditions attributable to current year tax positions 39,619 3,440 2,027\nAdditions attributable to prior year tax positions 2,199 27,994 32,569\nReductions attributable to prior year tax positions \u2014 \u2014 (85,513)\nReductions attributable to statute expiration \u2014 \u2014 (9,821)\nBalance at end of period $ 138,367 $ 96,549 $ 65,115\nIf we were to prevail on all positions taken in relation to uncertain tax positions, $138.4 million of the unrecognized tax benefits would ultimately benefit our effective tax rate.\n"}
{"page": 169, "text": "We recognize interest and penalties related to unrecognized tax benefits in our provision for income taxes. We recognized expense of $5.2 million, $9.5 million and benefits of $2.1 million for interest and penalties related to unrecognized tax benefits in our Consolidated Statements of Operations for the years ended August 31, 2026, 2025 and 2024, respectively, and a related $20.9 million and $15.8 million interest payable on our Consolidated Balance Sheets as of August 31, 2026 and 2025, respectively.\nIn December 2021, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) introduced a framework known as Pillar Two, aimed at ensuring large multinational enterprises pay a minimum 15% level of tax on income earned in each jurisdiction in which they operate. The OECD also issued administrative guidance, including transition rules and safe harbor provisions to support the implementation of the global minimum tax. Pillar Two legislation has been enacted or substantively enacted in certain jurisdictions where we operate. We have assessed our potential exposure to Pillar Two income taxes. Based on this assessment, there was no material impact on our consolidated financial statements for fiscal 2026. We will continue to monitor and evaluate any potential future implications for our consolidated financial reporting. Cash income tax payments, net of refunds, consisted of the following:\n2026 2025 2024\n(Dollars in thousands)\nFederal $ 10,258\nState (5,366)\nIowa (5,385)\nMinnesota (3,309)\nCalifornia 2,687\nLouisiana (1,752)\nOther states 2,393\nInternational 411\nTotal $ 5,303 $ 77,070 $ 129,065\nF-25 Table of Contents\nNote 12 Equities\nPatronage and Equity Redemptions\nIn accordance with our bylaws and by action of the Board of Directors, annual net earnings from patronage sources are distributed to consenting patrons following the close of each fiscal year and are based on amounts using financial statement earnings. The cash portion of 